Day 23: LangChain Models

Models are a crucial part of the framework.
They let you interact with language models and embedding models.

Model types:
1. Language models: text input and text output.
   A. LLMs
   B. Chat models
2. Embedding models: text in, vector data out.
   Help with semantic search and RAG based applications.

Language models:
Text is the input. Text is the output.

Types:
1. LLM: general purpose models. Can be used anywhere (text gen, code gen, and more).
   Becoming old. In LangChain they are being removed slowly.
   Trained from books, articles, and similar data.
   No memory of previous conversation.
   No role assignment like "act like a doctor".
2. Chat models: best for chatbots. Specialized models.
   Used in AI agents, chatbots, and more.
   Optimized for multi turn conversation.
   Trained with data like books and forums such as Reddit.
   Remember previous conversation.
   Can assign roles.

ChatGroq basic call:
from langchain_groq import ChatGroq
from dotenv import load_dotenv
load_dotenv()
llm = ChatGroq(model="llama-3.3-70b-versatile")
result = llm.invoke("what is the capital of nepal")
print(result.content)

Temperature: how much creativity to add. Range from 0 to 1.5+.
Math like tasks: use less, like 0.0 to 0.5.
Creative tasks: use higher, like 0.7 to 1.5+.

Max tokens: the model will not use more tokens than this limit.
Also print response_metadata and usage_metadata.

Open source models:
Free AI models you can download and modify.
Deploy without restrictions from a central provider.
Download the model. Use it how you want. Modify it. Deploy it. Full access.
Good data privacy. You can fine tune it.
Famous ones: Llama, Gemma, DeepSeek.
Find them on Hugging Face (multimodal, text generation, and more).

Ways to use Hugging Face:
1. Using the API from Hugging Face
2. Running locally

Disadvantages:
Huge hardware requirements.
Setup complexity.
Lack of RLHF: no fine tuning with human feedback in many cases.

.env:
HUGGINGFACEHUB_ACCESS_TOKEN = "ACCESS TOKEN"

Hugging Face API:
from langchain_huggingface import ChatHuggingFace, HuggingFaceEndpoint
from dotenv import load_dotenv
load_dotenv()
llm = HuggingFaceEndpoint(repo_id="id of the repo we want", task="text-generation")
model = ChatHuggingFace(llm=llm)
result = model.invoke("what is the capital of nepal")
print(result.content)

Hugging Face local:
from langchain_huggingface import ChatHuggingFace, HuggingFacePipeline
llm = HuggingFacePipeline.from_model_id(
  model_id="id of the repo we want",
  task="text-generation",
  pipeline_kwargs=dict(temperature=0.5, max_new_tokens=100),
)
model = ChatHuggingFace(llm=llm)
result = model.invoke("what is the capital of nepal")
print(result.content)

Embedding models turn text into embedding vectors.
OpenAIEmbeddings with dimensions: lower dimensions mean lower cost.
embed_query for one string.
embed_documents for a list of strings.
Document search: embed documents and query, then cosine_similarity to find the best match.
